<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haute-Loire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a> in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region, with its prefecture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy-en-Velay</a>. Created in 1790, it covers the upper valley of the Loire and most of the old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velay">Velay</a>, with parts of Auvergne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gevaudan">Gévaudan</a>. It is a land of volcanic plateaux, pasture and forest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haute-loire.jpg" alt="Haute-Loire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KlausFoehl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont_M%C3%A9zenc03_2016-05-20.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, which means Upper Loire, is a landlocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> surrounded by the departments of Loire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardeche">Ardèche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozere">Lozère</a>, Cantal and Puy-de-Dôme. The Loire rises in its southern part and flows north, creating a wide valley with ranges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massif-central">Massif Central</a> on either side. The highest point is Mont Mézenc at 1,753 metres, and the average elevation is 719 metres. Part of the north lies in the Livradois-Forez Regional Natural Park, a protected area of farmland and woodland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known inhabitants were hunter-gatherers, later followed by pastoralists. The Gaulish tribe of the Vellavi later controlled the region. Between 462 and 477 it was incorporated into the Visigothic Kingdom, ending Roman political control. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a> in 507, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velay">Velay</a> came under Frankish rule. In about 928 the area became a fiefdom of the Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and later came under the Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>.</p><p>Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> is one of the original 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, on 4 March 1790. Two thirds of it, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy-en-Velay</a>, is the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velay">Velay</a>, and its distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> had allowed it a great deal of autonomy. During the Second World War French partisans, aided by Allied supply drops, liberated the department. Le Puy-en-Velay was besieged by mid-August 1944, and on 18 August Germans trying to escape in a convoy of fifty trucks were ambushed. The town fell the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy-en-Velay</a> is the prefecture and the largest town, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> had 228,654 inhabitants in 2023. The cathedral of Le Puy is a gathering place for pilgrims before they set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, and it has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1998. Another pilgrimage site is a 12th-century chapel on a rocky pinnacle, reached by 268 steps. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> du Motier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-lafayette">Marquis de Lafayette</a>, was born in 1757 at the Château de Chavaniac.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haute-Loire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Vellavi held the volcanic uplands of the upper Loire, in the modern <b>Haute-Loire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/vellavi">Vellavi</a></li></ul></section>
